  <p>For the period from {{ start_date }} to {{ end_date }}, the {{ fund_name }} increased by {{ percentage_increase }}%, fueled by gains from U.S. equity markets. The S&P 500 gained {{ sp500_gain }}% for the 12-month period, compared to gains of {{ comparison_gain }}% and {{ comparison_gain_2 }}% from the international developed MSCI EAFE Index and the MSCI Emerging Markets Index, respectively. The U.S. outperformed as the economic expansion extended its 10th year, while growth expectations in international markets fell. The U.S. experienced double-digit returns across the portfolio, with an inflow of {{ inflow_amount }} in new capital. The biotech NASDAQ Biotech Index (0.2%) and energy (-13.2%) in negative territory. However, markets were volatile, as uncertainties surrounded a trade war with China and a potential U.S. recession weighed heavily on U.S. investors at times. In March 2019, the yield curve inverted prior to the U.S. Federal Reserve lowering interest rates by {{ fed_rate_change }} bps. In June, benefiting fixed income investors (Bloomberg Aggregate Bond Index +7.9%).</p>

  <p>In fiscal year {{ fiscal_year }}, the endowment returned {{ return_percentage }}%, outperforming the median institution in the Cambridge Associates (“CA”) College & University Universe. These results were driven by excess returns across the portfolio generated from world-class investment managers, as well as positive contributions from the portfolio’s asset allocation. Due to the long-term nature of the endowment, Dartmouth seeks diversified sources of return to manage risk and drive superior returns throughout a market cycle, and remains focused on sustained outperformance over long time periods. For the 10 years ending June 30, 2019, Dartmouth’s endowment generated an annualized return of {{ annualized_return_10y }}%, compared to a return of general peer equity/non-peer bond benchmark over the same period. Over the past 20 years, Dartmouth’s endowment has generated an annualized return of {{ annualized_return_20y }}%, and therefore has succeeded in meeting its primary objective of generating investment returns in excess of the spending distribution rate plus inflation. Dartmouth’s endowment continues to rank in the top quartile of colleges and universities as reported by Cambridge Associates over the trailing 1, 3-, 5-, 10-, and 20-year time periods.</p>

